  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 238 PM EDT Tue Sep 1 2026\n\n- Increased chances for showers and thunderstorms today and tonight. \n\nHave raised precipitation chances significantly during this time. \nThunderstorms overnight have a chance of being severe with damaging \nwinds and large hail being the main threat.\n\nLatest radar mosaic shows elevated showers with a few thunderstorms\nextending from the OH Valley, through SW Lower MI, and as far west\nas IA. The models are not handling this well at all. This activity\nappears to be associated with a subtle mid/upper level shortwave \ntrough noted in water vapor imagery and local VWPs. Given the unbroken\nchain of convection extending well upstream, PoPs look in order through\nthis afternoon for at least our southwest forecast area. The rest of\nthe forecast area will need to be watched for an escalating heat \nrisk this afternoon.\n\n\n- Thunderstorms, some severe, expected overnight\n\nMany of the 12Z Convection Allowing Models (CAMs) such as the HRRR, \nNAMNest, and RRFS have come into much better agreement regarding \nconvective evolution tonight and this consequently has appeared in \nthe 12Z HREF. We expect a greater than 50 chance of thunderstorms to \ndevelop or move into the forecast area around or shortly before \nmidnight tonight. The parameter space is impressive, with effective \nshear greater than 40 knots and MUCAPE in excess of 3000 J/kg. A \nlong and generally straight hodograph would support splitting \nsupercells capable of producing large hail. Not surprisingly, there \nare some SARS sounding analogs associated with significant hail to \nback this up. This all being said, the convective mode could end up \nleaning more towards multicell clusters instead of uncontaminated \nsupercells which would reduce the large hail threat somewhat.\n\nRegarding the wind threat, ML guidance such as NadoCast tends to favor\nthis much more than other hazard types. Damaging wind is a plausible\nconcern, because although there will be a nocturnal/marine boundary\nlayer induced inversion, some models generate substantial DCAPE, and\nwhen it comes to supercells with deep mesocyclones (as this environment\nsupports), there can be an added vertical non- linear dynamic component\nto assist with downward momentum transfer.\n\nAs noted in the WPC outlooks, there is a marginal threat for excessive\nrainfall. Precipitable water certainly is impressive but rainfall amounts\nwill likely be curtailed by a combination of progressive cell motion,\nthe presence of at least some hail, and reduced sub-cloud RH.\n\nModels suggest the focus for showers and thunderstorms will shift \nnorthward towards sunrise Wednesday morning. \n\n\n- Severe thunderstorm chances Wednesday and Thursday\n\nPredictability regarding thunderstorm specifics drops off rapidly early\nin the forecast, but confidence is high that we will remain in an environment\nsupportive of strong to severe thunderstorms. Persistent strong conditional\ninstability during this time is noted in the ECMWF ensemble mean, which\nshows MUCAPE around 3000 J/kg Wednesday dropping to a still respectable\n2000 J/kg by Thursday. Effective shear also consistently remains in\n40 knot territory during this time.\n\nBoth SPC SWODY2 and GEFS ML guidance show the greatest chance (Slight)\nof severe convection over southeastern Lower MI and touching our forecast\narea with a continued westward marginal risk area covering most of the\nforecast area. The main threats in play would be wind and hail and\nthis would be most likely to occur in the afternoon. A marginal risk\nfor excessive rainfall will continue as well. The other thing to watch\nwill be Heat Indices exceeding 100F Wednesday afternoon towards I-94.\n\n\n- Mostly afternoon thunderstorm chances Friday into Labor Day weekend\n\nThe upper pattern transitions to a sharp ridge just upstream, which \nnormally would be a dry pattern. However, in this particular case, conditional\ninstability supportive of showers and thunderstorms is expected to remain\ninto Sunday at least. The best chances for precipitation will be in\nthe afternoons/evenings as diurnal destabilization becomes a more\ncritical ingredient than before and favorable timing with transient\nupper PV maxima will be an added plus.",
